INT. BASEMENT REC ROOM - PING-PONG TOURNAMENT - NIGHT

Wood paneling and low ceiling. A single table under a bare bulb. Folding chairs hold a dozen onlookers, cans of soda in hand. A chalkboard bracket hangs on the wall, with SUNNY and BOOKER circled at the bottom.

SUNNY, 20s, a wiry woman in a headband and a retro track jacket, bounces the ball on her paddle. BOOKER, 40s, a big, slow man in a cardigan, wipes his hands on his pants. MILO, 12, the referee, sits on a stepladder with a whistle and a clipboard. FRANCESCA, 50s, Booker's wife, holds a thermos at courtside.

MILO
Nine to eight, Sunny.

SUNNY
I know.

BOOKER
I know.

They say it together. Both flinch.

SUNNY
I know the score.
^BOOKER
Let's finish this.

MILO
Serve.

Sunny serves. Booker slams it back. The ball skims the net and drops onto Sunny's side, where it rolls off the table's edge and under a chair.

FRANCESCA
Point, Booker!

MILO
Nine to nine. Final game point.

The crowd murmurs. Sunny retrieves the ball, blows on it, and tosses it to Booker.

SUNNY
Your serve.

BOOKER
You sure?

SUNNY
I like to win clean.

Booker grins. He rolls his shoulders, steps back, and lets the ball fly. Sunny returns it with a snap. It arcs high. Booker drives it down in a smash that rings on the table. Sunny dives, paddle outstretched, and flicks it back from the floor.

The ball hangs in the air. Everyone holds their breath. It lands, clips the edge, and drops.

MILO
Point... Sunny.

The room erupts. Sunny leaps up, fists raised. Booker bows his head, laughs, and holds out a hand.

BOOKER
Well played.

SUNNY
You almost had me.

FRANCESCA
(to Sunny)
He always almost has them.
